<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-lines" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Lines</title>

  <p>
    <term>Line</term><fn>Line is undefined.</fn> is short for straight line.
    Take a fixed <term>plane</term><fn>Undefined term</fn>, once and for all,
    and consider just lines that lie in it. The plane and the lines are
    <term>sets</term><fn>Undefined term</fn> of <term>points</term><fn>Undefined term</fn>.
    Each line is a <term>subset</term> of the plane, i.e. each element of a line is a point
    of the plane. Each line is endless, extending forever in both directions. Each line has
    infinitely-many points. The points on a line can be taken to be ordered along the line
    in a natural way. As a consequence, given any three distinct points on a line, exactly
    one of them lies <term>between</term> the other two. Points that are not on a given line
    can be said to be on one or other <term>side</term> of the line. The sides of a line are
    sometimes referred to as <term>half-planes</term>.
  </p>

  <convention xml:id="conv-point-line-notation">
    <title>Notation</title>
    <p>
      We denote points by roman capital letters <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, etc.,
      and lines by lower-case roman letters <m>l</m>, <m>m</m>, <m>n</m>, etc.
    </p>
  </convention>

  <p>Axioms are statements we will accept as true.<fn>An <term>axiom</term> is a statement
  accepted without proof, as a basis for argument. A <term>theorem</term> is a statement
  deduced from the axioms by logical argument.</fn></p>

  <axiom xml:id="ax-two-points">
    <title>Two Points Axiom</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is exactly one line through any two given points.
        (We denote the line through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> by <m>AB</m>.)
      </p>
    </statement>
  </axiom>

  <definition xml:id="def-line-segment">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The line <term>segment</term> <m>[AB]</m> is the part of the line <m>AB</m> between
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> (including the endpoints). The point <m>A</m> divides the line
        <m>AB</m> into two pieces, called <term>rays</term>. The point <m>A</m> lies between
        all points of one ray and all points of the other. We denote the ray that starts at
        <m>A</m> and passes through <m>B</m> by <m>[AB</m>. Rays are sometimes referred to
        as <term>half-lines</term>.
      </p>
      <p>Three points usually determine three different lines.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-collinear">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If three or more points lie on a single line, we say they are <term>collinear</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-triangle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> be points that are not collinear. The
        <term>triangle</term> <m>\triangle ABC</m> is the piece of the plane enclosed by
        the three line segments <m>[AB]</m>, <m>[BC]</m> and <m>[CA]</m>. The segments
        are called its <term>sides</term>, and the points are called its
        <term>vertices</term> (singular <term>vertex</term>).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-lines">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-lines-axiom">
      <statement>
        <p>How many straight lines can be drawn through two given points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Think about the Two Points Axiom.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>Exactly one.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>By the Two Points Axiom, there is exactly one line through any two given points.
        So there is exactly one line through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-lines-collinear">
      <statement>
        <p>Points <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m> all lie on the line <m>m</m>.
        Are they collinear?</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Look up the definition of collinear.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>Yes.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>Three or more points are collinear if they all lie on a single line.
        Since <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m> all lie on line <m>m</m>, they are collinear.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-lines-segment-vs-ray">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain the difference between the line segment <m>[AB]</m> and the ray <m>[AB</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Think about which one has two endpoints, which has one, and which continues forever.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>The segment <m>[AB]</m> is the finite part of line <m>AB</m> between <m>A</m>
        and <m>B</m>. The ray <m>[AB</m> starts at <m>A</m> and continues forever through
        and beyond <m>B</m>.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>The line segment <m>[AB]</m> is the part of line <m>AB</m> between <m>A</m> and
        <m>B</m>, including both endpoints. It has a definite finite length <m>|AB|</m>.
        The ray <m>[AB</m> starts at point <m>A</m> and passes through <m>B</m>, continuing
        forever in that direction. A ray has one starting point but no endpoint.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-lines-triangle-sides">
      <statement>
        <p>Triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> has vertices <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
        Name its three sides.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Each side is a line segment connecting two of the three vertices.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>The three sides are <m>[AB]</m>, <m>[BC]</m>, and <m>[CA]</m>.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>By definition, triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> is enclosed by the three line
        segments <m>[AB]</m>, <m>[BC]</m>, and <m>[CA]</m>. These segments are its three sides.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
